Popular password management app 1Password <a href="https://1password.com/blog/update-on-nov-2026-security-incident">today said</a> it experienced a security breach earlier this month. 1Password says some customer names and email addresses were accessed, but no customer vault contents were compromised.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2022/05/1password-deal-blue.jpg" alt="" width="2250" height="1266" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-846338" /><br/>
On November 19, 1Password saw indications that an employee's GitHub account had been breached and began investigating. The company says the breached GitHub account was used to access source code and push malicious code to backend services, with the code downloading some customer data. Here's a list of what the hacker was able to get:<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li>Names and email addresses customers use to sign into 1Password</li><br/>
 	<li>Account and user identifiers, and account creation and last sign-in times</li><br/>
 	<li>Public keys</li><br/>
 	<li>Secure Remote Password protocol parameters (1Password says this can't be used for account access)</li><br/>
 	<li>Tokens in emailed links for invitations, provisioning, and account recovery</li><br/>
 	<li>Other non-secret account data related to 1Password's internal architecture, like version numeric identifiers and file numeric identifiers</li><br/>
 	<li>1Password source code, which 1Password says does not include customer data or user secrets</li><br/>
 	<li>Access to 1Password's CI/CD system, which it says has infrastructure-related secrets for the service to work</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
According to 1Password, there is no impact to its services, availability, or product security. Customer vaults and the items in them were not accessed, nor were the keys that protect vaults. The hacker did not have access to account recovery keys or 1Password apps and browser extensions. 1Password vaults are encrypted and protected by keys on the user end, so content is not accessible to 1Password or anyone who hacks the 1Password servers.<br/>
<br/>
The biggest risk comes from the token breach. Tokens can be used for account recovery, but they only work when paired with an SSO sign-in or a six-digit code sent to a user's email address. Codes have now been changed from six numeric digits to 10 alphanumeric characters to make them more difficult to brute force.<br/>
<br/>
The affected GitHub account has been disabled, and the attacker's token was revoked. Internal credentials that may have been exposed have been revoked. Internal GitHub, CI/CD, and endpoint controls have been "tightened," with new detection methods for similar attacks. The company found no internal evidence of user account takeovers or compromise using the exposed account provisioning tokens, and it has hired independent cybersecurity experts to validate its response.<br/>
<br/>
1Password users who had a token accessed were notified, but it is unclear if 1Password has sent notices about the breach to all users. While the blog post is titled as "update on" a security incident, it does not seem that the breach was disclosed when 1Password first learned of it six days ago.<br/>
<br/>
1Password will not contact users to ask for an account password or Secret Key, and the information should be entered only in the official 1Password app or official website. It recommends all users be cautious about phishing attempts like emails, texts, and calls that mention 1Password or account recovery.<br/>
